 ## Background
-Differentiable rendering has evolved from volume rendering techniques to support modern neural graphics. Early work focused on volume rendering equations, where opacity $\alpha$ is computed by integrating density along the ray: $\alpha_i = 1 - \exp\left(-\int G_i(x) dx\right)$ [S1, unofficial]. Recent advancements integrate these principles with Gaussian Splatting, enabling real-time differentiable rasterization for tasks like robot learning and 3D reconstruction [S1, unofficial][S2]. Fourier Splatting extends this by using generalized Fourier encoded primitives, adapting power window functions to polar coordinates for scalable radiance fields [S2].
